# ============================================
# TRAVELPLAN — STAGING ENVIRONMENT TEMPLATE
# See README.md "Staging environment" for the full setup checklist.
# ============================================
# This file has 3 sections that go to 3 different places — do not lump them
# together in one dashboard. Mixing backend and VITE_* vars into one Railway
# service is exactly how a stray VITE_MAPBOX_ACCESS_TOKEN ended up set on the
# production API service (harmless there, but a sign of copy-paste error).

# ============================================
# SECTION A — Railway "staging" Environment, service "travelplan-api"
# ============================================

# envalid only accepts development|test|production — there is no "staging"
# value. Staging runs with NODE_ENV=production (same runtime behavior as
# prod); the environment is distinguished by which Mongo/Redis/secrets it
# points to, not by NODE_ENV.
NODE_ENV=production
# Dedicated var so /health and /ready can report which environment answered,
# and so Pino logs carry env without overloading NODE_ENV. Read via
# `appEnv` in backend/src/config/env.ts.
APP_ENV=staging
PORT=3000

# ============================================
# DATABASE — Atlas, matching production's actual topology (verified:
# production's MONGO_URI is mongodb+srv://, not a Railway-hosted Mongo
# plugin — don't default to provisioning one for staging just because it's
# a one-command `railway add --database mongo`, that puts staging on a
# different vendor than prod). Use a dedicated Atlas database user scoped
# via "Specific Privileges" to readWrite on `travelplan_staging` only (not
# "any database" / not the local-dev or production user) — Database
# Access → Add New Database User → Specific Privileges → readWrite @
# travelplan_staging (leave collection blank). Atlas Network Access must
# allow Railway's egress — Railway has no static IP by default, so this
# usually means 0.0.0.0/0 (Allow Access from Anywhere) unless you've paid
# for Railway's static IP add-on.
# ============================================
MONGO_URI=mongodb+srv://travelplan_staging:password@cluster0.your-project.mongodb.net/travelplan_staging?retryWrites=true&w=majority

# ============================================
# REDIS — separate from production. Use whichever shape your staging Redis
# needs; REDIS_URL (if set) overrides the HOST/PORT/PASSWORD trio below.
# Production today uses the HOST/PORT/PASSWORD form from a Railway Redis
# service in the same project — mirror that unless you have a reason not to.
# ============================================
REDIS_HOST=
REDIS_PORT=6379
REDIS_PASSWORD=
# REDIS_URL=redis://default:password@host:6379

# ============================================
# AUTHENTICATION (Clerk) — reuse the Clerk TEST instance already used by E2E
# (CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY_E2E / CLERK_SECRET_KEY_E2E in GitHub secrets), or a
# dedicated staging dev instance. Never the production instance.
# Register a SEPARATE webhook endpoint for staging
# (https://<staging-api>/api/webhooks/clerk) — the production webhook secret
# will fail signature verification against a staging host.
# ============================================
CLERK_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_your_staging_clerk_secret_key_here
CLERK_WEBHOOK_SIGNING_SECRET=whsec_your_staging_webhook_secret_here
# Required by the backend too (clerkMiddleware() is mounted globally and
# needs it explicitly) — same value as VITE_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY in Section C.
CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_test_your_staging_clerk_publishable_key_here

# ============================================
# AI & EXTERNAL APIS — Gemini/Mapbox/Google Places have no separate test
# tier; use dedicated staging keys created in the same GCP projects
# (travelplan-486522 for Places, gen-lang-client-0544187342 for Gemini) so
# usage/killswitch can be told apart from prod, even though quota is shared.
# Stripe DOES have test mode — always use sk_test_ here.
# ============================================
GEMINI_API_KEY=your_staging_gemini_api_key_here
MAPBOX_ACCESS_TOKEN=pk.your_staging_mapbox_token_here
GOOGLE_PLACES_API_KEY=your_staging_google_places_api_key_here
OPENWEATHER_API_KEY=your_openweather_api_key_here
# OPENWEATHER_BASE_URL=https://api.openweathermap.org/data/2.5
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_your_staging_stripe_secret_key_here
# stripe.service.ts constructs `new Stripe()` at import time — this must
# NOT be left empty, or the API/worker will throw STRIPE_NOT_CONFIGURED on
# every billing call. Register a separate staging webhook endpoint
# (https://<staging-api>/api/webhooks/stripe) for its own whsec_ secret.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_your_staging_stripe_webhook_secret_here

# ============================================
# FEATURE FLAGS — both default OFF under NODE_ENV=production. Set explicitly
# if you want to exercise these flows on staging.
# ============================================
# ENABLE_GOOGLE_CALENDAR_SYNC=true
# VIP_EMAILS=you@example.com

# Worker-only in practice (gates the insight-scraping cron in worker.ts),
# harmless if also set on the API.
SERPER_API_KEY=

# ============================================
# CORS / FRONTEND
# ============================================
# Set to the Vercel branch alias for `develop` — confirmed live at
# https://travelplan-web-git-develop-lam-phams-projects-44c4677b.vercel.app
# (Vercel assigns this automatically to every deployment of the `develop`
# branch; no custom domain or extra Vercel config needed to get it).
# (Stripe checkout success/cancel URLs derive from this too.)
FRONTEND_URL=
# Usually left empty — cors.ts already allow-lists any *.vercel.app origin,
# which covers Vercel's Preview deployments for `develop` without needing
# this list.
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# ============================================
# SECTION B — Railway "staging" Environment, service "travelplan-worker"
# ============================================
# Copy EVERY var from Section A onto the worker service too, unchanged.
# worker.ts imports backend/src/config/env.ts exactly like the API does, so
# envalid's fail-fast applies identically — missing any of the 5 required
# vars (MONGO_URI, CLERK_SECRET_KEY, CLERK_WEBHOOK_SIGNING_SECRET,
# CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY, GEMINI_API_KEY) crashes the worker at boot.
#
# What actually matters where, if you want to reason about it instead of
# just copying everything:
#   - Worker-only:  SERPER_API_KEY (gates the insight-scraping cron).
#   - API-only (harmless-but-inert on worker): PORT, FRONTEND_URL,
#     CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS, STRIPE_SECRET_KEY, STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET,
#     OPENWEATHER_API_KEY, OPENWEATHER_BASE_URL.
#   - Both need: MONGO_URI, all 3 Clerk vars, GEMINI_API_KEY, Redis vars,
#     MAPBOX_ACCESS_TOKEN, GOOGLE_PLACES_API_KEY, NODE_ENV, APP_ENV,
#     ENABLE_GOOGLE_CALENDAR_SYNC, VIP_EMAILS.
# Recommendation: keep them identical to Section A, like production does —
# divergence buys nothing here and risks a boot crash from a forgotten var.

# ============================================
# SECTION C — Vercel project "travelplan-web", target = Preview ONLY
# ============================================
# These are BUILD-TIME vars set in the Vercel dashboard scoped to Preview.
# Do NOT copy the Production target's values here — today Preview and
# Production share identical values, meaning the `develop` preview talks to
# the production API and production Clerk. Unscoping them from Preview (and
# adding these staging values instead) is the actual point of this file.
#
# Vercel builds every deployment (including `develop`) via the root
# vercel.json → `npx turbo run build --filter=@travelplan/web` → `vite build`, i.e.
# **mode=production**. frontend/web/.env.staging and `npm run build:staging`
# (`vite build --mode staging`) are LOCAL-ONLY — Vercel never runs them.
#
# VITE_API_URL MUST include the /api suffix: lib/axios.ts uses it as
# baseURL and every caller passes a path without /api
# (apiClient.get("/trips")); assistant.api.ts has no fallback at all.
VITE_API_URL=https://<staging-api>.up.railway.app/api
VITE_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_test_your_staging_clerk_publishable_key_here
# Both names: useMap.ts still reads only VITE_MAPBOX_TOKEN, while
# config/env.ts prefers VITE_MAPBOX_ACCESS_TOKEN — set both until that's
# unified (see .claude/reference/tech-debt.md).
VITE_MAPBOX_ACCESS_TOKEN=pk.your_staging_mapbox_token_here
VITE_MAPBOX_TOKEN=pk.your_staging_mapbox_token_here
VITE_OPENWEATHER_API_KEY=your_openweather_api_key_here
VITE_OPENWEATHER_BASE_URL=https://api.openweathermap.org/data/2.5
# Lets IS_STAGING / IS_PROD tell staging apart from a real production build.
VITE_ENV=staging
# Must match ENABLE_GOOGLE_CALENDAR_SYNC / VIP_EMAILS above if you want the
# calendar sync UI to actually work end to end on staging.
# VITE_ENABLE_GOOGLE_CALENDAR_SYNC=true
# VITE_VIP_EMAILS=you@example.com
